apers and theses have been published and defended, and significant additional funding has been granted to several teams. CCBIO-based books have been published. Thanks to Agnete Engelsen, Liv Cecilie Vestrheim Thomsen and Erling Høivik for chairing the CCBIO Junior Scientist Symposia this year, an important arena for training that should be supported by all investigators and teams; thanks to all who supported our courses, scientific seminars and the annual symposium; thanks to Elisabeth Wik for organizing the INTPART-funded CCBIO Scientific Writing Seminar which was held recently and became a huge success with around 90 participants; and to all administrative and scientific personnel for your great efforts. Please note the dates for the CCBIO Annual Symposium coming up in 2018 (May 23-24), and on May 22, Klaus Pantel, who is a member of the International Faculty, will host a satellite symposium on Liquid Biopsy (also at Solstrand). Read this Newsletter and do not miss any important dates. We look forward to 2018 and the second term of the CCBIO journey. Merry Christmas and best wishes for 2017 to all of you! Best regards, Lars A. Akslen, Director', '', 'This is localized at Haukeland University Hospital, the Radiology Department. The purpose of the center is to strengthen cross-disciplinary research activities related to the state-of-the-art imaging equipment, i.e. high field MRI, CT and pre-/clinical PETCT. Earlier this year, BFS for a total of three research projects that would be linked to the center. The funding has now been announced. 25 researchers submitted project proposals, and two of the accepted investigators were CCBIO collaborators at the Department of Clinical Medicine and at the Department of Biomedicine.', '', '2', 'Watnick presented a novel mechanism governing tumor initiation and cancer stem cell function. The results of his group’s trials show that a novel signaling axis, involving Notch1, SIRT1 and p53 functionally defines tumourinitiating epithelial cells as well as cancer stem cells. . Watnick and Akslen, editors of the new CCBIO book "" "" gave the book to CCBIO PhD candidates during the pizza get-together afterwards.', '', 'This was a seminar that came about through an exchange program The seminar was fully booked shortly after announcement. Master students, PhD students, postdocs and senior researchers attended, both from UiB’s Faculty of Medicine and other faculties, and from Haukeland University Hospital participated. The auditorium, which takes 90, was full, The lecturers, Christine Møller, assistant editor of APMIS and an experienced lecturer in medical and scientific writing, and Randy Watnick, assistant professor at the Vascular Biology Program, Harvard Medical School, proved to complement each other in an excellent way, fitting the CCBIO aim of excellency at all levels! , including some useful writing tips and good advice.', '', 'RECENT CCBIO IN THE MEDIA 28.11.17, National Foundation of Cancer Research (USA), Parasite Killer Found to be Effective Cancer Treatment Candidate, Karl-Henning Kalland. 27.11.17, E24 Børs, Bergen Bio ber Helsetilsynet om godkjennelse, BerGenBio. 25.11.17, BT, Helsetilsynet ba BerGenBio stanse forskningsprosjekt, BerGenBio. 23.11.17, the Australian Hospital Healthcare Bulletin, Gut instinct: tapeworm drug fights prostate cancer, Karl-Henning Kalland. 20.11.17, ScienceNordic, Tapeworm drug fights prostate cancer, Karl- Henning Kalland. 17.11.17, Colorectal Cancer Canada, Tapeworm Medicine Stops Prostate, Colon Cancer Cells from Growing, New Study Finds, Karl-Henning Kalland. 17.11.17, Prostate Cancer News Today, Tapeworm Medicine Stops Prostate, Colon Cancer Cells from Growing, New Study Finds, Karl-Henning Kalland. 17.11.17, Pan European Networks, The Nexus: efficient approaches, Roger Strand. 17.11.17, DotEmirates, Existing parasite drug may fight prostate, colon cancer, Karl-Henning Kalland. 16.11.17, European Pharmaceutical Review, Tapeworm drug fights prostate cancer